  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel. Its documented rule says: 'The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.' The two reply texts are:\nA: {\"value\": \"cat\", \"span\": \"cat\", \"document\": \"The cat sat.\"}\nB: {\"value\": \"cat\", \"span\": \"cat  sat\", \"document\": \"The cat sat.\"}\nFor A the rule requires GROUNDED, because the span 'cat' occurs in the document and the value 'cat' is in the span. For B the rule requires SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT, because with no relaxation the span 'cat  sat' (two spaces) does not occur in the document 'The cat sat.' (one space). The function, however, normalises whitespace before checking the span, so it returns GROUNDED for both. What would make me wrong: if _grounding_kernel returns SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT for B, or if the documented rule is read as allowing whitespace normalisation as part of verbatim matching.",
